[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: ZEF INTERFACE — public system surface
UID: ZEF-INTERFACE-DOC
VERSION: 1.0.0
DATE: 2026-10-10
PREFIX: ZEF-IF
ROOT: False
OPTIONS:
  ENABLE_MID: False
  MARKUP: RST

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
INTERFACE items describe the surface through which a caller uses the system:
types, operations, inputs, conditions, limits, results, and assumptions. They
may repeat caller-visible preconditions and exposure rules where needed for a
complete surface; WHAT items contain the formal behavioral contract. The
detailed public C surface is included below in rich-text blocks and is maintained
in this document as the authoritative interface baseline.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Common surface and configuration

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-001
TITLE: Public runtime structures and reserved members
STATEMENT: >>>
The public headers provide complete C structure definitions for ``ZMessagePool``,
``ZMessagePoolSet``, ``ZMsgHandle``, ``ZMsg``, ``ZMailbox``, ``ZMachine``, ``ZReactor``, ``ZKernel``,
``ZTopicTable``, ``ZClock``, ``ZTimer``, and ``ZPort``. An application can
therefore allocate these objects automatically, statically, or as members of
an application-owned structure. Framework-owned members are private by intent:
the documentation marks them as reserved, and applications shall access them
through the public operations rather than reading or modifying them directly.
This is a documentation and usage boundary, not an opaque-handle or
allocation-indirection requirement.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-001
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-007
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-002
TITLE: Results and panic boundary
STATEMENT: >>>
``ZResult`` covers exactly the expected observations ``Z_OK``, ``Z_EMPTY``,
``Z_NOT_RUNNING``, and ``Z_NOT_FOUND``. Fatal conditions use
``ZPanicReason`` and
``ZPanicInfo`` through the application-supplied ``z_panic`` function. The
function is non-returning and is responsible for safe-state handling,
diagnostic capture, and stop-or-reset policy. It is an application-wide
function, not a Port member or per-object callback, so every framework object
can use the same fatal-error boundary without carrying a callback field.

.. code-block:: c

   typedef uint8_t  ZBool;
   typedef uint16_t ZTopic;
   typedef uint16_t ZReactorId;
   typedef uint8_t  ZPriority;
   typedef ZEF_TICK_TYPE ZTick;

   typedef enum {
       Z_OK = 0,
       Z_EMPTY,
       Z_NOT_RUNNING,
       Z_NOT_FOUND,
   } ZResult;

   typedef enum {
       Z_PANIC_INVALID_ARGUMENT,
       Z_PANIC_CONFIGURATION,
       Z_PANIC_NO_MEMORY,
       Z_PANIC_MAILBOX_FULL,
       Z_PANIC_OWNERSHIP,
       Z_PANIC_LIFECYCLE,
       Z_PANIC_STATE,
       Z_PANIC_OVERFLOW,
       Z_PANIC_PAYLOAD_SIZE,
       Z_PANIC_INTERNAL
   } ZPanicReason;

   typedef struct {
       ZPanicReason reason;
       void const *object;
       uintptr_t detail;
       char const *file; /* optional in release builds */
       uint32_t line;    /* optional in release builds */
   } ZPanicInfo;

   typedef void (*ZPanicHandler)(ZPanicInfo const *info);
   _Noreturn void z_panic(ZPanicInfo const *info);

The exact enum width is a configuration decision, but numeric values remain
stable within one build. Mailbox overflow, pool exhaustion, invalid arguments,
invalid lifecycle transitions, ownership violations, state violations, and
counter overflow are panic conditions rather than ordinary results. Valid
pointer-producing operations return non-null pointers; an unavailable pointer
is a panic condition.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-006
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-013
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-003
TITLE: Caller-owned storage
STATEMENT: >>>
Runtime objects and Mailbox slot arrays are supplied by the caller. Complete
public runtime structures may be automatic, static, or embedded in an
application-owned structure. Framework-owned structure members are reserved by
documentation and are not application storage. The host core has no
general-purpose allocation path.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-002
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-004
TITLE: Configuration surface
STATEMENT: >>>
Configuration names cover reactor slots, topic count, message-pool count,
state depth, queue capacities, tick width, and pool or claim counter widths.
The configuration header publishes the valid range for each setting and
rejects invalid values with a build-time diagnostic. Each selected width uses
an exact-width integer type where layout is observable.
It exposes ``ZEF_POOL_ALIGNMENT``, ``ZEF_TOPIC_BITMAP_WORD_BITS``, and
``ZEF_MAX_TIMER_SPAN``; pool alignment is at least ``alignof(max_align_t)``
and ``ZEF_MAX_TIMER_SPAN`` is less than half the representable ``ZTick`` range.
The selected tick type is exposed as ``ZEF_TICK_TYPE``; its default is
``uint32_t``.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-003
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Detailed public C surface

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Common conventions
------------------

The public headers are C11-compatible, use exact-width integer types where
width is observable, and expose complete caller-allocated runtime structures
whose framework-owned members are reserved by documentation. Functions return
``ZResult`` only when the outcome is an
expected part of normal control flow. Fatal conditions invoke the user-supplied
panic handler and do not return. Output parameters are written only on success.
Pointers to sealed Messages are read-only in all application-facing APIs.

The exact enum width is a configuration decision, but numeric values remain
stable within one build. ``Z_EMPTY``, ``Z_NOT_RUNNING``, and ``Z_NOT_FOUND``
describe expected state observations. Mailbox overflow, pool
exhaustion, invalid arguments, invalid lifecycle transitions, ownership
violations, state violations, and counter overflow are panic conditions, not
ordinary ``ZResult`` values.

The application supplies the ``z_panic`` function. It makes the hardware safe,
preserves useful diagnostic state, and does not return. It shall be safe to
call from either thread or interrupt context, shall not block or allocate,
and shall handle fatal conditions raised by the ISR-safe posting and
publication operations. A debug handler may stop the target or save a memory
and register dump; a release handler normally requests an MCU reset. The
framework uses the Port's interrupt-state operation before invoking the handler
when required and treats a returning handler as a fatal application error.

The normal operating assumption is that a correctly configured and correctly
written application never reaches the panic handler. Panic handling is the
fail-stop boundary for violated assumptions, not recoverable application
control flow. Every pointer-producing operation has a non-null result on a
valid call; a pointer result that cannot be provided is a panic condition.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Configuration and storage
-------------------------

The build supplies limits such as ``Z_MAX_REACTORS``, ``Z_MAX_TOPICS``,
``Z_MAX_STATE_DEPTH``, ``Z_MAX_MESSAGE_POOLS``, and the selected counter widths.
The public configuration header validates their ranges with static assertions,
publishes ``ZEF_POOL_ALIGNMENT`` and ``ZEF_MAX_TIMER_SPAN``.

Runtime objects and Mailbox slot arrays are caller-owned. The public headers
define complete runtime structures, so applications may use stack, static, or
embedded storage without size/alignment query APIs. Framework-owned members are
reserved by documentation and are not application state. The host core does
not call a general-purpose allocator.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Mailbox surface
---------------

The Mailbox owns a caller-provided ring of read-only Message pointers. The ring
has monotonic head and tail indices and never allocates.

``post`` and ``post_front`` seal and consume a building ``ZMsg``, transferring
its creator claim to the destination. ``postpone`` retains an already sealed
Message for the destination Mailbox before enqueueing it for later handling. A
full Mailbox invokes the panic handler with
``Z_PANIC_MAILBOX_FULL`` and does not return. A successful ``take`` transfers
the Mailbox claim to the caller; an empty ``take`` returns ``Z_EMPTY``.
Removal releases each removed dynamic Message claim and returns the number
removed.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Machine and state-node surface
------------------------------

State nodes are static descriptors. The Machine owns the current node and the
application context but not descriptor storage.

Dispatch walks parent links until a handler handles the Message or requests a
transition. Transition processing leaves the source path in reverse order,
excluding the common ancestor, changes the current node, enters the target
path in hierarchy order, and follows ``initial`` links. A self-transition
leaves and re-enters the current node before following its initial links.
Dispatch performs no allocation. A transition target that is null or outside
the validated graph invokes the panic handler with ``Z_PANIC_STATE``.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Reactor and Kernel surface
--------------------------

The Reactor binds a Machine and Mailbox to a scheduler identity. Registration
assigns a zero-based endpoint index exactly once.

Registration and initialization failures invoke the panic handler. A Reactor
identity remains registered for the Kernel lifetime; Phase 1 has no stop or
remove operation and no stopped or removed Reactor state.

The Kernel owns the runnable bitmap and registry. Its finite host surface is:

``z_kernel_run_one`` dispatches at most one Message, selects the highest-priority
runnable Reactor, breaks equal-priority ties by ascending zero-based Reactor
identity, updates runnable state after the Mailbox changes, and returns
``Z_NO_WORK`` when the runnable bitmap is zero. ``run_forever`` calls the Port
idle operation whenever ``run_one`` reports no work.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Topic publication surface
-------------------------

Topic tables contain one subscriber bitmap per topic and use a critical section
for subscription changes and publication snapshots.

Publication seals and consumes the building ``ZMsg`` before taking the
subscriber snapshot. Recipients are processed in descending priority order;
equal-priority recipients are processed in ascending zero-based Reactor
identity order. If any recipient is full, publication invokes the panic
handler with ``Z_PANIC_MAILBOX_FULL`` and does not return. Partial-delivery
recovery is not provided after the fatal condition; recipients processed before
the panic retain their claims. On normal return, each processed recipient
obtains one claim, the creator claim is consumed (and released when there are
no recipients), and the operation returns the delivery count.

Unsubscribing a topic that is not currently subscribed returns ``Z_NOT_FOUND``.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Clock and Timer surface
-----------------------

Timers are caller-owned nodes in a delta list. ``z_timer_init`` transfers one
dynamic Message claim to the Timer; the caller must not release that claim
after initialization. The Timer retains ownership while armed and releases it
when disarmed with no pending delivery. Each pending dynamic delivery has its
own claim. Static immutable Messages require no claim bookkeeping.
``z_timer_init_topic`` creates a timer-owned immutable zero-payload Message for
the supplied topic without using a Message pool. Timer expiration posts a
Message and never invokes an application handler from tick context.

An armed Timer cannot be armed again; attempting to do so invokes the panic
handler with ``Z_PANIC_STATE``. A period of zero means one-shot delivery. A
periodic Timer expiring at tick T is reinserted for T plus its period using
unsigned modular tick arithmetic. A zero delay expires on the next tick, and a
Timer armed during processing of tick T is not eligible until a later tick.
Timers due on the same tick are posted in arm order; delays and periods are
limited to the configured less-than-half-range maximum.
Stopping unlinks it immediately and returns ``Z_NOT_RUNNING`` when it was
already stopped. Cancellation stops the Timer and removes only that Timer's
pending Messages; it does not remove unrelated deliveries of the same Message
value.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Message ownership and typed payload surface
--------------------------------------------

Message construction uses a mutable ``ZMsg`` handle. It is valid only between
``z_message_begin`` and the first consuming post or publication. The consuming
operation seals the Message before enqueueing it; the resulting ``ZMsgHandle``
is immutable and may be shared.

``z_message_begin`` creates one creator claim and returns a non-null pointer to
the complete mutable payload region. The payload size includes the complete
application payload; the allocator uses the envelope-plus-payload size to
select the smallest available block size that can contain the complete
Message, with caller-supplied order breaking equal-size ties. ``z_mailbox_post``,
``z_mailbox_post_front``, and ``z_topic_publish`` seal the ``ZMsg`` before
enqueueing or taking the subscriber snapshot, then consume the creator claim.
A normal delivery transfers the caller's claim; ``postpone`` retains an
additional claim for the destination Mailbox on an already sealed handle while
the caller keeps its existing claim. Pool exhaustion uses
``Z_PANIC_NO_MEMORY`` and does not return an exhausted result. Invalid
construction lifecycle violations also panic. Retain and release are no-ops
for static Messages, so generic receiver cleanup does not need to branch on
Message kind. Dynamic release reclaims the block only after the last claim.

Static Messages may be declared as immutable objects and are accepted by
read-only delivery APIs without pool bookkeeping.

``ZMsgHandle`` is a complete public structure whose framework-owned members are
reserved by documentation. Applications define the payload type rather than
reading or modifying those reserved members. In Phase 1, ``ZTopic`` is also the
payload type identifier, so each topic has one documented payload schema. The
payload size is stored in the Message envelope.

Posting seals the Message. A receiving handler reads the payload through the
const accessor:

Checked access invokes the panic handler with ``Z_PANIC_PAYLOAD_SIZE`` when the
exact size does not match. The application selects the expected type after
dispatching on the Message topic; a topic does not carry multiple incompatible
payload schemas.

When a payload contains a pointer to an external buffer, the buffer owner keeps
it alive and immutable for as long as any Message claim can be observed. Data
larger than one fixed payload uses a separate Message type or an
application-owned external buffer.
<<<

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Port contract
-------------

The host Port implements fake interrupt masking. The Cortex-M0 Port maps the
same contract to ``PRIMASK`` and ``PendSV``.

Critical leave restores the saved state. The Port is an internal framework
adapter; it does not own the application panic boundary and does not classify
application-callable API functions. Interrupt context legality for fixed-pool
Message construction, Mailbox, publication, scheduler, and timer operations is
recorded by ``ZEF-IF-041``. All fatal paths call the application-provided
``z_panic`` function.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Messages, pools, and Mailboxes

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-010
TITLE: Message construction and access
STATEMENT: >>>
The construction handle is mutable only before a consuming operation; the
sealed handle is const to callers. The construction and read surface is:

.. code-block:: c

   void *z_message_begin(ZMessagePoolSet *pool_set,
                         ZTopic topic,
                         size_t payload_size,
                         ZMsg *message);
   void *z_message_payload_mut(ZMsg *message);
   void *z_message_payload_mut_checked(ZMsg *message,
                                       size_t expected_size);

   ZTopic z_message_topic(ZMsgHandle const *message);
   size_t z_message_payload_size(ZMsgHandle const *message);
   void const *z_message_payload(ZMsgHandle const *message);
   void const *z_message_payload_checked(ZMsgHandle const *message,
                                         size_t expected_size);

   #define Z_MSG_BEGIN(pool_set_, topic_, Type_, msg_) \
       ((Type_ *)z_message_begin((pool_set_), (topic_), sizeof(Type_), (msg_)))

   #define Z_MSG_PAYLOAD(message_, Type_) \
       ((Type_ const *)z_message_payload_checked((message_), sizeof(Type_)))

   #define Z_MSG_PAYLOAD_MUT(msg_, Type_) \
       ((Type_ *)z_message_payload_mut_checked((msg_), sizeof(Type_)))

   typedef struct {
       uintptr_t address;
       uint8_t *buffer;
       size_t length;
   } ZIoRequest;

   ZMsg msg;
   ZIoRequest *request =
       Z_MSG_BEGIN(&message_pools, Z_IO_REQUEST, ZIoRequest, &msg);
   request->address = address;
   request->buffer = buffer;
   request->length = length;
   z_mailbox_post(&mailbox, &msg);

   void handle_io_request(ZMsgHandle const *message)
   {
       ZIoRequest const *request = Z_MSG_PAYLOAD(message, ZIoRequest);
   }

The typed construction and access macros validate the fixed payload size. A
valid call returns a non-null pointer; size mismatch and invalid lifecycle
conditions use the panic boundary. Read access to a dynamic sealed Message
requires the caller to hold a live claim; an absent or stale dynamic claim is
an ownership violation. Read access to a static Message requires only that the
caller-owned Message and payload remain alive and immutable.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-010
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-013
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-011
TITLE: Message ownership operations
STATEMENT: >>>
Ownership operations apply to sealed ``ZMsgHandle`` values. Normal post
transfers the caller claim; ``postpone`` adds a claim; dequeue transfers the
Mailbox claim to the receiver; final release returns the block to its
originating pool.

.. code-block:: c

   void z_message_retain(ZMsgHandle const *message);
   void z_message_release(ZMsgHandle const *message);

For a dynamic Message, retain adds one claim, release removes one claim, and
the final release returns the block to its originating pool. Dynamic claim
overflow, release without a dynamic claim, and post-seal mutation are panic
conditions. For a static immutable Message, retain and release are no-ops;
they do not inspect or change a claim count and never return the Message to a
pool. Callers may therefore use the same cleanup path for either Message kind
without first identifying which kind they received.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-011
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-012
TITLE: Message pool inputs and limits
STATEMENT: >>>
Pool initialization accepts caller storage, storage size, block size, and block
count. Storage shall be non-null, aligned to ``ZEF_POOL_ALIGNMENT``, and at
least ``block_size * block_count`` bytes. ``block_size`` shall be nonzero,
large enough for the private envelope plus the largest payload class assigned
to the pool, and a multiple of ``ZEF_POOL_ALIGNMENT``. ``ZEF_POOL_ALIGNMENT``
shall be at least the alignment required by ``max_align_t``.

The pool set surface is:

.. code-block:: c

   typedef struct {
       ZMessagePool *pools;
       uint16_t count;
   } ZMessagePoolSet;

   void z_message_pool_set_init(ZMessagePoolSet *pool_set,
                                ZMessagePool *pools,
                                uint16_t count);

Each element of ``pools`` shall have been initialized by
``z_message_pool_init``. The set retains the caller-owned array for the set's
lifetime; the array shall not be reordered or modified while Messages may be
allocated from the set.

A requested allocation includes the private envelope and payload. The message
service selects the smallest available block size that can contain that total;
equal block sizes are selected in caller-supplied array order. If no suitable
pool has a free block, the panic boundary is used.

.. code-block:: c

   void z_message_pool_init(ZMessagePool *pool,
                            void *storage,
                            size_t storage_size,
                            size_t block_size,
                            uint16_t block_count);

Exhaustion, invalid alignment, insufficient storage, invalid sizing, an empty
pool set, and an unavailable pointer use the panic boundary rather than
returning a null pointer or ordinary exhaustion result.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ZEF-WHAT-010
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: docs/api-requirements.sdoc

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ZEF-IF-013
TITLE: Mailbox operations
STATEMENT: >>>
Inputs include a non-null caller-owned pointer ring and nonzero capacity. The
ring shall not alias the Mailbox object or another live Mailbox ring. A Mailbox
may be assigned to a registered Reactor as its scheduler-visible inbox or may
remain unassigned as an application-owned local/deferred queue. The Mailbox
surface is:

.. code-block:: c

   void z_mailbox_init(ZMailbox *mailbox,
                       ZMsgHandle const **slots,
                       uint16_t capacity);
   void z_mailbox_post(ZMailbox *mailbox, ZMsg *message);
   void z_mailbox_postpone(ZMailbox *mailbox,
                           ZMsgHandle const *message);
   void z_mailbox_post_front(ZMailbox *mailbox, ZMsg *message);
   ZResult z_mailbox_take(ZMailbox *mailbox,
                          ZMsgHandle const **message_out);

   uint16_t z_mailbox_count(ZMailbox const *mailbox);
   uint16_t z_mailbox_capacity(ZMailbox const *mailbox);
   uint16_t z_mailbox_free(ZMailbox const *mailbox);
   uint16_t z_mailbox_min_free(ZMailbox const *mailbox);

   typedef ZBool (*ZMsgPredicate)(ZMsgHandle const *message, void *context);
   uint16_t z_mailbox_remove_if(ZMailbox *mailbox,
                                ZMsgPredicate predicate,
                                void *context);

Empty ``take`` returns ``Z_EMPTY`` without writing its output. Full enqueue
uses the panic boundary. ``post_front`` places the new Message immediately
before the current next Message; repeated front posts are therefore taken in
last-in-first-out order. ``remove_if`` evaluates its predicate once per queued
entry in delivery order, preserves the order of retained entries, returns the
number removed, and releases each removed dynamic Message claim. A null
predicate is an invalid argument and invokes the panic handler. The minimum
free-space observation is initialized to capacity and records the lowest free
space reached since Mailbox initialization. Operations on an unassigned local
Mailbox never update a Kernel runnable identity and never dispatch Messages
automatically. An application may defer a sealed Message received by a handler
with ``z_mailbox_postpone`` and later call ``z_mailbox_take`` when the
owning Machine reaches the appropriate state. For a dynamic Message, the
application then owns the returned claim and releases it after processing. For
a static Message, the application receives a reference with no claim; calling
``z_message_release`` after processing is still valid and is a no-op. The
``postpone`` name means that the sealed Message is retained for later handling;
the sealed Message storage is shared between the existing caller claim and the
new Mailbox claim for dynamic Messages, while static Messages simply share the
same reference. The payload is not copied.

.. code-block:: c

   ZMailbox deferred;
   ZMsgHandle const *deferred_slots[4];
   z_mailbox_init(&deferred, deferred_slots, 4);

   /* In a handler that must defer the current sealed Message. */
   z_mailbox_postpone(&deferred, message);

   /* In the application-selected state that is ready to process it. */
   ZMsgHandle const *deferred_message;
   if (z_mailbox_take(&deferred, &deferred_message) == Z_OK) {
       z_machine_dispatch(machine, deferred_message);
       z_message_release(deferred_message);
   }
<<<
